WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.790
PRABHAKAR RAGHAVAN: Hello,
everyone, and again, welcome.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:17.810
My name is Prabhakar, and I'm
responsible for the Google Apps

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.020
products.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:25.090
The journey I'm going to take
you on today goes back 10 years

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.230
when we [INAUDIBLE]
out to the world

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.830
what we then called Google
Apps for Your Domain.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.630
This was primarily centered
on our Gmail offering--

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.970
our flagship email
offering-- that's

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:41.560
used by over a billion
users today-- active users.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:47.370
But in time, we added
calendaring, unique cloud-based

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.430
real-time collaborative
editing, video conferencing,

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:56.650
and a host of other
suite services

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.180
that really rested in one
fundamental principle--

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.320
that people work together.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.920
They work better together in
the cloud collaboratively.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.880
And my word, what a
journey it's been.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.690
Today, we're thrilled
to have and honored

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:16.410
to have over two million
customers who depend on us

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:21.830
every day to enable their
employees to be successful.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:24.170
And as we work with
these customers,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.272
we've learned a
tremendous amount

00:01:28.272 --> 00:01:30.355
of what it takes to make
organizations successful.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.290
And with that, we have evolved.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:40.570
And I couldn't be more excited
to announce today that we

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.380
have evolved into G Suite.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:51.010
G Suite is an integrated
suite of applications designed

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.520
to enable workers to
collaborate better

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.830
in the cloud with the power of
Google's machine intelligence

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:01.410
to enable them to work smarter.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:06.940
And my team and I are
really excited to invest

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.600
in introducing G Suite to you.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.952
You'll get a lot more about
this in the weeks and months

00:02:11.952 --> 00:02:12.452
to come.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.430
But what does this really
mean for our customers

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:21.510
and for our products?

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.940
This industry has been fixated
on personal productivity

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.550
for three decades now,
and in the process,

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.290
a lot of good things
have happened.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.570
We've all created
tools that we use

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:36.490
every single day-- many tools
that we love to use, some

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:37.890
that we love to hate.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:42.620
But that's the current
plane at which we operate--

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.160
personal productivity.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.670
What G Suite aims to
do is to elevate that

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.980
beyond to a couple of levels.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.600
First-- and I'll
walk you through this

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.860
in the next few
minutes-- we want

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.470
to think about teams
as first class objects.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:01.820
What do teams do
in organizations?

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.450
How do they work together?

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:07.160
What journeys do they embark
on in their day-to-day lives?

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.000
And then from there,
we'll go on to what

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.945
it takes to make
organizations successful.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:18.890
So again, by thinking about
people-- the atomic units

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.870
in an organization.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:25.070
And I'm going to dwell
for the next few minutes

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:27.360
on a series of
announcements that I'm

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.730
really excited about
bringing to you today

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.940
on the power of
machine intelligence

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.040
enabling people to
be more successful

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.210
in your organizations.

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:44.940
A study by McKinsey estimates
that the average employee

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:49.490
really gets about two
productive, creative days

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:51.790
in a week.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.280
So what you ask are they
doing the other three days?

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.560
Well, they're searching
for information,

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:02.320
they're scheduling meetings,
they're triaging e-mail.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.930
Things that we think
of as mundane work.

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.840
That's where we
think that the power

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.230
of artificial
intelligence we can make

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:10.454
things dramatically better.

00:04:13.031 --> 00:04:14.655
The first example
I'm going to give you

00:04:14.655 --> 00:04:18.019
of something we're launching
now comes from Google Drive.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:22.420
Many of you are used to file
systems, to file repositories,

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:26.240
and I'm sure you can
identify with this journey

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.400
that people go through
over and over again.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.670
You go into your
file repository,

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.120
you scratch your head-- I
know it's in there somewhere.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.850
Do I search?

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:36.640
Do I browse?

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.490
Do I filter?

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.030
Do I try and guess
who gave me that file?

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:46.090
And on a good day, our studies
show that the average employee

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:50.570
spends 15 seconds each time
they come into a file depository

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.881
before they get to the
file they're looking for.

00:04:52.881 --> 00:04:54.130
And that's only on a good day.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.060
Lots of people don't find
what they're looking for,

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:57.935
and then they go off
and ask their colleagues

00:04:57.935 --> 00:05:00.970
and bug a whole bunch of
people across the organization.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:05.670
So we challenged our engineers
and said, we're Google.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.542
We need to do better than this.

00:05:07.542 --> 00:05:10.380
And this is what
they came up with.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:15.780
Quick Access uses
your activity patterns

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.810
to predictively serve
up to you the file

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:23.840
that you're about to need
just as you arrive in Drive.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:25.710
No searching, no
typing, no clicking.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.570
You come in there--
and I've been

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:29.790
using this feature
for a few weeks now,

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.520
and a lot of the
time, we get it right.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:33.728
And the best part of machine
intelligence and machine

00:05:33.728 --> 00:05:35.557
learning is as
the system learns,

00:05:35.557 --> 00:05:36.640
it gets better and better.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.110
So that's the first of
the unique announcements

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:40.110
I'm happy to make today.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.862
And Google Drive Quick Access
will give you your files

00:05:43.862 --> 00:05:45.070
even before you ask for them.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.630
All right, so you've
got the file you wanted.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.420
What do you want to do with it?

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.950
I'm going to walk you through a
series of machine intelligence

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:59.800
capabilities in our
document editors

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.240
that we collectively refer
to as Explore capabilities.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.740
The first of these
I'll do as a demo,

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:07.380
and then after
that, I'll walk you

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:08.671
through the other capabilities.

00:06:08.671 --> 00:06:10.680
And I'm joined by my
colleague Jeremiah

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:15.600
who's going to put up on
the screen a spreadsheet.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.680
So this is exploring Sheets.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:22.331
Can we have Jeremiah
on the screen, please.

00:06:22.331 --> 00:06:22.830
Great.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:28.840
So this is a spreadsheet of
somewhat fictitious sales data

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.710
from the last holiday season.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:35.120
And what we can bring
here is the power

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:36.810
of Google's natural
language processing.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:38.530
The problem we're
trying to solve

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:44.040
is a large fraction of people--
about a third of people

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.720
who use Sheets-- have no
idea what formulas are.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:47.720
They don't want to know.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:50.240
They want to do some analysis.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.240
They should be able to ask
questions and get the answers.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:59.350
So Jeremiah is now going to
ask a natural language question

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:01.740
just as an analyst might ask.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.700
So what's the average profit
margin for [? socks. ?]

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:09.470
And if the demigods are with
us, out pops the answer-- 0.45.

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.600
And what it did
underneath the covers

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.500
was to take that natural
language question

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:16.610
and transcribe it into
a formula that just

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:18.080
got executed against the Sheet.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:19.493
Let's try another
example Jeremy.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:27.585
What was the most popular
product on Black Friday?

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:33.200
Fair question, you might ask.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.600
And by comes the answer-- belts.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.030
Again, this is where
we're able to take

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.544
the power of natural
language processing,

00:07:41.544 --> 00:07:43.460
and Jeremiah, do you
mind showing the formula?

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:43.960
Great.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:47.250
So right there, he's showing
you the fairly complex formula

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:51.269
that got drawn out
of that fairly not so

00:07:51.269 --> 00:07:52.560
complex natural language query.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:56.930
Excellent.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.750
Thanks, Jeremiah.

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:00.816
If we could have the
main slides back please.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:04.820
Good.

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:06.706
Well, not quite.

00:08:06.706 --> 00:08:09.480
Could we switch back please.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.240
Excellent.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:15.080
I'm going now to briefly touch
upon the explored functionality

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:19.450
in a couple of other settings
in our document editors.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:23.740
Explore in Slides attacks
the following problem.

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:25.531
When you're creating
a presentation,

00:08:25.531 --> 00:08:27.030
you want to get
your message across.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:30.070
You want to get your
content in and deliver it.

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:34.419
You don't want to lose more
time formatting and polishing

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:37.460
and rounding the
corners of boxes.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:39.580
That's not what
you're here to do.

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:43.159
What Explore in Slides does
is you drop in your content,

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:45.280
and in a couple of
clicks, it automatically

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:48.230
formats it into a beautiful
presentation that's

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:51.200
going to have your boss thinking
that you stayed up all night

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.850
polishing the slides.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:55.240
Great.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:58.700
Very quickly, let me touch
upon Exploring Google Docs.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.880
Four years ago in 2012,
we launched a feature

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:05.840
that we called
Docs Research where

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:09.482
we coupled the Google search
engine into your Docs editor.

00:09:09.482 --> 00:09:10.940
And it's somewhat
gratifying to see

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:14.740
the world take notice and start
to do similar things right now,

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:18.170
but we decided to
go much further.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:22.490
You want to be able to compose
a document about Neil Armstrong,

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:26.050
and it starts to pull in content
about the Apollo 11 mission

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:29.270
from Google's gigantic
knowledge graph.

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:32.110
That is the power
of Exploring Docs.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.670
What we can do is bring
in related concepts

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:38.050
not only from the web,
but also documents

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:40.880
from your drive
and shared content

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:46.560
across your company's
repositories.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:47.860
Let me turn to my next example.

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:55.870
Calendars and scheduling have
been unchanged for 200 years.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:59.640
A calendar is a bunch
of boxes in a grid.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.042
And an assistant
is some poor soul

00:10:02.042 --> 00:10:03.500
who's hunched over
a terminal who's

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:07.320
moving those boxes around
all day for a living.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:08.860
This is the 21st century, folks.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:10.770
That's not acceptable.

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:13.560
So we decided to change that.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:16.680
Here's how Find Time
works launching today

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.570
in Android and iOS,
soon to come to the web.

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.810
You know that a bunch of
people have to get together.

00:10:25.294 --> 00:10:26.710
We haven't yet
gotten to the point

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:29.168
of guessing who those people
are, but let's say you tell us

00:10:29.168 --> 00:10:31.380
who those people are.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:35.190
We then figure out
automatically looking

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:39.440
at everybody's schedules,
understanding people's meeting

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:41.810
patterns, and finding
the best time for them

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:43.650
to meet to maximize
the probability

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:46.140
the meeting actually happens.

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:47.977
And in time, as
the system learns,

00:10:47.977 --> 00:10:49.560
you're going to get
better with things

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.750
like find the right
room for them to meet.

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:57.690
So the promise
here is scheduling

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:00.610
should become more and
more automated and less

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:05.820
and less reliant and prone
to human error and trouble.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:10.200
So as a result of
this, you're going

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.570
to start to see more
innovation in the space.

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:18.970
And in time, you shouldn't
have those poor souls

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:23.175
hunched over terminals
dealing with this stuff.

00:11:23.175 --> 00:11:26.970
All right, I'm going
to pause for a second

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:29.770
and reflect a little bit on all
of this machine intelligence

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:30.786
capability.

00:11:30.786 --> 00:11:33.520
I'm going to begin
with a confession.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:37.160
I worked in these
technologies for two decades,

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:38.760
and I will confess
that I did not

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:41.670
foresee that a day would
come in my lifetime

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:44.340
when computers would beat
the World Go champion.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:48.340
I did not foresee that
in a matter of months,

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:51.180
we could take those same
machine learning kernels

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:56.600
and turn it into an engine
that answers 10% of emails

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:58.360
automatically.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:01.510
So Smart Reply composes
responses to emails,

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:04.080
and 10% of the time, users
are perfectly happy using

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.000
what the machine has composed.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:10.030
I did not foresee that
in this generation,

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:13.030
we'd have machines that
could take images or videos

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:18.530
and annotate them, or real-time
cloud-based translation where

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:20.550
you hold up a phone
to a sign and it gets

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:23.550
translated into your language.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.250
And I attribute
this to the maniacal

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:27.220
focus our founders
Larry and Sergey

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:29.530
put in machine
intelligence and forced us

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:33.350
all to re-examine the
assumptions on which we're

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:35.510
building these products.

00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:39.820
Now, there's a second
point I want to make here.

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:41.750
There's a lot of
players out there

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:44.450
talking about artificial
intelligence being the future.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:46.630
And that's great.

00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:48.210
At Google, artificial
intelligence

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.490
is not the future, it's
current shipping reality.

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:54.740
And that's why we're able to
move from beating the World Go

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:57.470
champion 40 years
ahead of schedule

00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:00.280
to products that use exactly
the same technologies in months.

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:01.670
This is not a lab experiment.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.800
What it takes is a way of
life, a way of transcribing

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:08.080
a business problem into
a metric optimization

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:11.060
that machine learning then
turns into beautiful experiences

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:11.687
like these.

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:17.220
That's what I had to
say about individuals.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:21.590
Now let's uplift this to the
level of team productivity

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:25.390
because I believe this is
going to become increasingly

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:28.740
important in the years to come.

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:33.850
So the point here is we need to
think of teams as first class

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:36.700
citizens and how
they go about doing

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:40.580
their work every single day.

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:45.020
And today, I'm pleased
to make two announcements

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:50.760
in this realm, both going into
early adopter program today.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:54.100
The first we call Team Drives.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:55.180
So what's Team Drives?

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:02.475
Wow, I didn't realize
it would be so popular.

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:10.120
So the idea of Team Drives is
to get away from the notion

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:12.640
that content is
owned and managed

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:17.810
by individuals because at some
level, people join a group.

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:21.520
And when they leave, the
content gets orphaned.

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:23.660
You don't want that.

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.870
So Team Drive is a
setting where a team

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:30.700
is a first class object in terms
of its ownership of content.

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:34.870
Teams manage content, assign
content to other people,

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:39.180
and all of this is fully dynamic
as the identity of a team

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.580
keeps changing because
people can join a team,

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:45.948
people can exit a team, and
all of that leaves team content

00:14:45.948 --> 00:14:46.448
unaffected.

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:52.210
The second announcement
I want to bring you

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:54.530
is a new Hangouts
experience for video.

00:14:57.140 --> 00:15:00.620
When you make a phone call,
you don't pick up your phone

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:04.040
and say, is that person
on Verizon or Vodafone?

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:05.690
Do they have an iPhone, Android?

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:09.050
You just dial their number
and you're off to the races.

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:13.235
It's that same fluidity we want
to bring to video conferences.

00:15:13.235 --> 00:15:15.610
It should be just as seamless
to make a video conference.

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:17.980
So the new Hangouts
experience, also entering

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:22.030
early adopter program today,
offers the following features.

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:28.330
First, as always with Google,
no downloads, no plug-ins,

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:31.350
nothing extraneous.

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:33.970
Simple link-based
video conferencing.

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:34.850
No identities.

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:40.700
So you can easily communicate
with partners, with customers,

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:43.330
with anybody outside
your domain-- candidates.

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:46.040
Things should be [INAUDIBLE]
every single day.

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:49.280
Moreover, every meeting
by default-- and this

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:51.810
has been one of our
biggest requests

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:55.150
from customers-- by
default, every meeting

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:56.289
has a phone number.

00:15:56.289 --> 00:15:58.080
So if you're caught in
the airport security

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:00.010
line at the time of
the video conference--

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:03.230
an experience I'm sure every
one of you can relate to-- you

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:06.510
don't have to find a nice
place where you're visible.

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:09.400
Just pick up the
phone and join in.

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:11.700
As simple as making
a phone call.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:15.050
Expect to hear more
from us in this area.

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:20.090
But the new Google
Hangouts video experience

00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:23.656
is an early adopter
also starting today.

00:16:23.656 --> 00:16:26.830
All right, so I've
talked about people.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:30.470
I've talked to about the next
layer of teams, and as I said,

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:33.490
you should expect to hear more
announcements in this area

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:36.030
from us with other products.

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:41.000
Let's now up-level it to the
success of the organization.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:44.860
And really, the goal here is
to break down the information

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:46.475
silos in enterprise.

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:53.680
Three months ago, we launched
a unique cloud-based search

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:59.010
and assist capability
that we call Springboard.

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:02.150
What this gives
you is the ability

00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:04.270
to harness all of the
content in your enterprise,

00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:06.430
whether first party
or third party.

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:10.890
Bring it into a single
index, and first of all,

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:14.460
provide secure
search, search that's

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:15.940
sensitive to reorganizations.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:19.099
So you have a reorg,
and instantly,

00:17:19.099 --> 00:17:22.130
content you're not privy
to is hidden from you.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:23.609
Or if you become
privy to content,

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:26.730
conversely, you can access
that content through the search

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:27.690
functionality.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:31.550
So this is technically
pretty challenging,

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.640
and I'm thrilled to say my
team has pulled this off.

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:36.100
But there's more.

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:37.960
You're all familiar
with Google Now

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:43.770
on the consumer side in similar
fashion with enterprises.

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:47.980
This capability proactively
provides you assist capability.

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:51.450
So when you're walking
into a meeting,

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:53.785
the assist capability
surfaces a document and says,

00:17:53.785 --> 00:17:57.260
here's the document. you're
going to need for that meeting.

00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:00.080
And if you look really
carefully at the screenshot,

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:01.740
the document being
suggested here

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:04.370
comes not from Google but
from Box, with whom we just

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:06.480
signed a partnership
to be able to service

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:09.086
exactly this kind of content.

00:18:09.086 --> 00:18:10.960
So it doesn't have to
be just Google content.

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:12.709
It's whatever's relevant
to your business.

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:20.430
So that, in summary,
is G Suite--

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:23.600
a suite of applications
designed to make your business

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:25.270
run smarter.

